Abstract
Particulate matters with different sizes (PM2.5 and PM10) in the air have become a supreme environmental concern to human health globally. Herein, the magnetic-luminescent Cu//Tb dual metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) incorporated side-by-side nanofibrous (SBS-NFs) membrane has been successfully fabricated via the electrospinning technique with the selfdesigned nozzle for the first time. The fabricated SBS-NFs membrane is composed of CuMOF/PAN at one side and Tb-MOF/PAN at another side with their individual characteristics. The Cu-MOF was used to improve the filtration efficiency and reduced the pressure drop by enhancing the electrostatic interaction, whereas, Tb-MOF was incorporated to investigate the PM adsorption process through the changes in luminescence intensity. Compared to polyacrylonitrile nanofibers (PAN NFs), Cu//Tb dual MOFs incorporated SBS-NFs membrane showed an enhanced filtration efficiency $(90.2\%)$ and a reduced pressure drop $\left(60.7\mathrm{Pa}\right)$ when sodium chloride (NaCl) aerosols with particle size less than $300\mathrm{nm}$ were used for testing. As a result of the filter test using car exhaust, Cu//Tb SBS-NFs membrane still maintains $591\%$ PM removal efficiency for ${>}30$ hours. In addition, it is confirmed that the luminescence intensity over the PM adsorption time was linearly weakened. Therefore, Cu//Tb dual MOFs incorporated SBS-NFs membrane can be used for a variety of applications.
Keywords: Metal-organic framework; Side-by-Side nanofibers; Luminescence; PM capture; electrospinning method; Cu//Tb Dual MOFs
## 1. Introduction
Air pollution has been noted as a serious problem that causes a significant effect on human health, visibility, and environmental pollution [1-3]. Each year, more than two million people die from the illness attributable to indoor and outdoor pollution, especially in developing countries, according to the World Health Organization (WHO) reports [4, 5]. Particulate matters (PM) are the most hazardous among solid pollutants. According to particle size, PM is divided into PM10 (particle diameters below $10\upmu\mathrm{m}$ ) and PM2.5 (particle diameters below 2.5 $\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . Compared to large particles, small particles can be transported a farther distance and stay in the air for longer. In addition, PM has a high specific surface area, enabling them to easily carry poisonous and harmful substances in polluted air. Therefore, the inhaled PM will settle in the human lungs and can lead to a variety of diseases including lung cancer [1, 2, 5-7]. Air filtration is one of the techniques that can effectively remove these particles from the pollutant air. The air filter has been widely used for industrial gas removal and it includes a variety of products, such as disposable masks, indoor air purification systems, and automotive engine filters. Porous film filters and fibrous filters have been commonly used. The latter have high PM filtration efficiencies and are easy to produce different shapes, thus they have been attracting more attention for air filter applications. Usually, the filtration efficiency improves as the fiber diameter decreases. When the fiber diameter is reduced to the nanoscale, the filtration efficiency increases because of the increased contact between the PM and the fiber surface [8]. Nanofibrous membranes have many advantages including a high specific surface area, porous structure, and flexible functionality [1, 2, 4, 9-11]. Electrospinning is a common and simple method for fabricating nanofibers for air filter applications [6, 10, 12-16]. However, the reduced diameters and high packing densities of nanofibrous membranes always result in dense structures, leading to high-pressure drop, which not only clogs the filters and shortens the filter lifetime but also uses more energy. It is important to decrease the pressure drop while simultaneously maintaining high filtration efficiency. In general, a nanofibrous filter with a low-pressure drop can be achieved by designing a membrane structure that incorporates nanoparticles such as a metal-organic framework (MOF) [1, 7, 17, 18].
A MOF is a type of porous crystalline material composed of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands. It has a high specific surface area, an adjustable pore size, and tunable functionality that can be effective in a variety of applications, which are beneficial for capturing pollutants. In addition, metal ions and defects on the surface of the MOF provide a positive charge that can increase the PM removal efficiency by improving electrostatic interactions [7, 12, 19-21]. MOF filters can catch pollutants through the following three mechanisms: (1) binding to an open metal site on MOFs, [22] (2) interacting with functional groups of MOFs and/or polymers, (3) an electrostatic interaction with MOF nanocrystals [6]. However, in practical applications, powder crystalline materials have many problems, such as clogging pipes or recycling. Therefore, electrospun nanofibrous membrane with high specific surface area and excellent mechanical properties are attractive properties for various applications, such as air filtration and oil/water separation. For instance, Zhang et al. [6] showed that nanofibers with high MOF content improved PM2.5 and PM10 filtration efficiencies to $88.33\%$ and $89.67\%$ , respectively, and maintained that performance for 48 hours. Bian et al. [12] fabricated a ZIF-67 based nanofiber filter membrane by electrospinning using $\mathrm{PAN/Co(AC)}_{2}$ and 2-methylimidazoleethanol solutions. The incorporation of ZIF-67 increased the filtration efficiency from $74.5\%$ to $87.2\%$ and maintained it for over $99\%$ of a long-term efficiency test (30 days). However, almost all the reports are about to silent over the estimation time that when the filters need replacing as there are no visible signs even filters are fully saturated. In general, it can also be hard to determine the efficiency of the filter as a function of time because efficiency is not only dependent on exposure time but also with concentration of particles in the atmosphere. Therefore, it is imperative to know easily when the air filter needs replacing.
In this study, fabrication of the Cu//Tb dual MOFs incorporated side-by-side nanofibrous (Cu//Tb SBS-NFs) membrane with multifunctionality is reported for the first time using an electrospinning technique with a specially designed nozzle. Therefore, the novelty of this report lies mainly in the idea of designing the side by side nanofibrous filters with multiple functionalities. The electrospinning was carried out using two different solutions (CuMOF/PAN/DMF and Tb-MOF/PAN/DMF) in the two different plastic syringes with the same feeding rate and applied voltage at the same time with the single nozzle. Typical side by side fiber was divided into two parts, one to ascertain the Cu-MOF, the other the Tb-MOF. CuMOF introduced into one side of nanofibers can improve the filtration efficiency and reduce the pressure drop by inducing electrostatic interactions and while Tb-MOF of the other side can show the PM adsorption process over time with the naked eye on nanofibers through changes in luminescence intensity. Further advantages of the present SBS-NFs membrane can be the following: (1) bicomponent fibers can increase the surface area that facilitates the high PM capture efficiency and (2) improvement of the mechanical properties of side-by-side fibers membrane by the synergistic effect of each fiber. Therefore, the Cu//Tb SBS-NFs membrane with luminescent property has potential as the PM adsorption detector.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Terbium (III) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , $99.999\ \%$ ; CAS number 13451-19-9), 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\bf~\underline{~}{~H_{3}B T C}}$ ; CAS number 554-95-0), triethylamine (TEA, $299.5~\%$ ; CAS number 121-44-8), and polyacrylonitrile (PAN, $\mathtt{M_{n}}\mathrm{=}150,000$ ; CAS number
25014-41-9) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Sodium acetate trihydrate 1 $\mathrm{(CH_{3}C O O N a\cdot3H_{2}O}.$ , $98.5\%$ ; CAS number 6131-90-4) was purchased from Duksan Chemical. Copper acetate monohydrate $\mathrm{(Cu(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}\cdot H_{2}O}.$ $98\%$ ; CAS number 6046-93-1) was purchased from Junsei. N, N-dimethyl formamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ ; CAS number 68-12-2), and ethyl alcohol $95\%$ ; CAS number 64-17-5) were purchased from Samchun. All chemicals and solvents were used without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Cu-MOF
Cu-MOF was synthesized according to the previously reported method [6]. Briefly, $1.008\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ was dissolved in $16~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF with $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ of triethylamine, and $\ensuremath{1.72\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{Cu(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}\cdot H_{2}O}$ was dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. The two solutions were combined in a mixture of $144~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF, $200~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethyl alcohol and $200~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and magnetically stirred at $300~\mathrm{rpm}$ for an hour. The precipitation was washed with DMF, and ethyl alcohol. Then the product was dried at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The experiment was conducted at a room temperature of $22{-}25~^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ and relative humidity of $40\%$ .
## 2.3. Synthesis of Tb-MOF
Tb-MOF was synthesized according to the previously reported method [30]. Briefly, $0.52{\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ $\phantom{+}0.252\textrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ , and $0.488\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O N a}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ were dissolved in a mixture of $96~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and $48~\mathrm{mL}$ of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ by stirring at $300\mathrm{rpm}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ . This solution was maintained at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 20 hours. The precipitation was washed with DMF, and ethyl alcohol. Then the product was dried at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The relative humidity during the experiment was about $40\%$ .
## 2.4. Preparation of spinning solution
Two different kinds of spinning solutions were prepared to fabricate Cu//Tb SBS-NFs. One spinning solution was composed of Cu-MOF, PAN, and DMF. Another spinning solution was a mixture of Tb-MOF, PAN and DMF. Briefly, each MOF was dispersed in DMF by stirring. After that, PAN was dissolved in the MOF/DMF dispersion by stirring for 12 hours. For comparison, a spinning solution for the fabrication of Cu/Tb MOF-derived single nanofibers (Cu/Tb SNFs) was also prepared. The compositions and contents of these spinning solutions are listed in Table 1. Side-by-side nanofibrous membranes with different MOF concentrations were prepared (Table S1).
## 2.5. Fabrication of [Cu/PAN//Tb/PAN] side-by-side nanofibrous membrane
Cu//Tb SBS-NFs were fabricated using a self-designed nozzle, as indicated in Figure 1. The two different spinning solutions were loaded into each syringe and connected with the selfdesigned nozzle by silicon tube. The electrical conductivity and viscosity of each solution were measured before electrospinning to stably form a side-by-side shape (Table S2). The comparable electrical conductivity and different viscosity of each solution ensured the stability of the jet. The voltage was maintained at $15\mathrm{kV}$ and the tip-to-collector distance was $15\ \mathrm{cm}$ . Both feeding rates of the two spinning solutions were $0.006~\mathrm{mL~min^{-1}}$ . As-spun fibers were
collected in a cylindrical rotating collector with a diameter of $10\ \mathrm{cm}$ and a length of $30~\mathrm{cm}$ .   
Cu/Tb SNFs were also fabricated using a single nozzle under the same conditions.
## 2.6. Characterization techniques
The morphology of the materials was investigated using field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, SUPRA 40VP, Carl Zeiss Ltd., Ger.) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2200FS, JEOL Ltd., Japan). TEM coupled with energy-dispersive Xray spectroscopy (TEM-EDS) was used to analyze the chemical composition of Cu//Tb SBSNFs. The incorporation of MOF into nanofibers was investigated by using X-ray diffraction (XRD) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The crystalline structure of the materials was detected using XRD (MAX-2500, RIGAKU, Japan) with a $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\mathfrak{a}$ radiation source at 40 kV and $100~\mathrm{{mA}}$ in a 2θ range from $5^{\circ}$ to $40^{\circ}$ at a scan speed of $2^{\circ}\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ . The elemental composition of the prepared samples was examined by XPS with a K-alpha X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (Thermo Scientific, Nexsa XPS system, UK). The specific surface area was measured with the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method (UPA-150, ASAP2010, AutoporeIV, USA).
The filtration efficiency and pressure drop were measured using an automatic filtration testing unit (TSI 8130, USA). The tester could deliver electrically neutral monodispersed NaCl aerosol particles with sizes less than $300\mathrm{nm}$ . The particles passed across the filter over a valid testing area of $100\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The NaCl aerosol filtration experiments were carried out at ambient temperature under a continuous airflow velocity of $5.3\ \mathrm{cm\s^{-1}}$ (airflow rates of $32\mathrm{~L~min^{-1}}$ ). Testing samples were prepared by electrospinning on a PET nonwoven fabric. The fabric has a negligible filtration performance, with a filtration efficiency of $11\%$ and a pressure drop of $4.9\mathrm{Pa}$ . The overall performance of the air filter was evaluated by quality factors related to the filtration efficiency and pressure drop. The quality factor (QF) is defined as follows:
$$
Q F=-\frac{l n\left(1-\eta\right)}{\varDelta P}
$$
where $\boldsymbol{Q}\boldsymbol{F}\left(\mathrm{Pa}^{-1}\right)$ is the quality factor of the materials, $\eta\ (\%)$ is the filtration efficiency, and ${\varDelta}P$ $\mathrm{(Pa)}$ is the pressure drop.
The long-term and field test performance is very important for the practical application of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs. Car exhaust was used to test the filtration efficiency of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs in practical environments, which was previously reported [31]. The temperature of the car exhaust usually ranged from 50 to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Herein, the thermal properties of the materials were tested by thermogravimetric analyses (Perkin-Elmer Co., USA) which were performed in a platinum pan from 30 to $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a rate of $10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ under a nitrogen flow. Furthermore, Cu//Tb SBSNFs must be kept stable under strong blowing conditions, such as exhaust with a gas velocity of $2{\cdot}3\ \mathrm{m/s}.$ Therefore, mechanical properties were measured by a universal testing machine (UTM, LR5K Plus; Lloyd Instruments LTD., UK) under a crosshead speed of $5\mathrm{mm}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The testing samples were prepared in the form of standard dumbbell shapes according to ASTM standard D882. The long-term filtration test was maintained for 30 hours to confirm the efficiency for long-term and particles with different size. The filtration efficiency is calculated as follows:
$$
E_{f}=\frac{C_{1}-C_{2}}{C_{1}}*100\
$$
Where $E_{f}$ is the filtration efficiency of the materials is, $C_{1}$ is the particle number concentration before filtration, and $C_{2}$ is the particle number concentration after filtration. The PM particle number concentration was measured by a PM particle counter (CEM, China). To show the PM adsorption process with the naked eye in a practical application, the luminescent behavior was measured by spectrofluorometric with a stopped-flow (RMS-1000, Olis, Inc., USA).
## 2.7. Statistical Analysis
All data are presented as a mean $\pm$ standard deviation $(\mathbf{n}=3)$ ). One-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) was used to analyze the significance of the difference between groups. P-value of less than 0.05 was considered statistically significant.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology and structure
The morphology of the materials is shown in Figure 2a-d. The MOFs are nanoscale-sized to ensure their compatibility with and dispersion in the nanofibers (Figure S1 and S2) [32]. As shown in Figure 2a-c, the surface of PAN NFs is smooth but the surfaces of Cu/Tb SNFs and Cu//Tb SBS-NFs are rough. The dispersion of MOF particles on the fiber as indicated clearly on the images could make the MOF incorporated fibers were rougher than the non-MOF incorporated PAN NFs. In addition, MOFs are found well dispersed in the nanofibers without obvious aggregation. The FE-SEM images of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs demonstrate that side-by-side nanofibers were successfully prepared (Figure 2c). The cross-section of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs shows that each Cu//Tb SBS-NFs consists of two nanofibers assembled side by side with diameters similar to each other. The diameter for the individual nanofibers of Cu and Tb SBSNFs is approximately $1.2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ .
To demonstrate the side-by-side structure of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs, a TEM-EDS line scan analysis was performed (Figure 2d), where Cu and Tb represent the Cu-MOF and Tb-MOF, respectively. From the TEM-EDS line scan analysis, It is found that the typical fiber is divided into two parts, one to ascertain the Cu, the other the Tb, confirming the successful fabrication of bicomponent Cu//Tb SBS-NFs.
Figure 2. FESEM images of (a) PAN NFs, (b) Cu/Tb SNFs, (c) Cu//Tb SBS-NFs; and (c’) corresponding cross-section image, and (d) TEM image and EDS line scan analysis of $\mathrm{{Cu//Tb}}$ SBS-NFs.
As shown in Figure 3a, the Cu//Tb SBS-NFs exhibit the major diffraction peak at $2\boldsymbol{\uptheta}=12^{\circ}$ corresponding to Cu-MOF and the peaks at $2\uptheta=8$ and $11^{\circ}$ corresponding to Tb-MOF [33] suggesting the well incorporation of each component in the nanofibers. The additional broad diffraction peak observed between 15 and $20^{\circ}$ is associated with the characteristic amorphous diffraction peaks of PAN NFs. The XRD spectra of the Cu-MOF and Tb-MOF are in good agreement with previous reports [34-37]. The XRD spectra confirm the successful spinning of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs on the PET nonwoven fabric for the filtration test (Figure S3).
The surface chemical composition of the prepared membranes was analyzed by XPS. The PAN NFs shows the peaks at binding energy 284.2, 531.9, 401.3 eV corresponding to elements C 1s, N 1s, and O 1s, respectively (Figure 3b). However, Cu//Tb SBS-NFs show the additional peaks at $930.4~\mathrm{eV}$ and $153.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ which are corresponding to Cu 2p and Tb 4d, respectively [38]. Similarly, the peaks at 1240-1290 eV are attributed to Tb 3d. Thus, the XPS spectra confirm that the Cu-MOF and Tb-MOF are successfully incorporated in the PAN NFs.
## 3.2. Filtration test
The surface area and porosity of the single nanofibers and side-by-side nanofibers were investigated by $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption at $77\mathrm{K}$ . As shown in Figure 4a, all the curves show a type IV isotherm with a series of typical adsorption behaviors, exhibiting the characteristics of mesopores within the materials [6, 14]. $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms demonstrate that the incorporation of MOFs largely improves the porosity and surface area of PAN NFs. The BET surface area of the samples highlights the degree of MOF exposure on the nanofiber surface. Herein, the surface area of PAN NFs is found $1.3\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ which increased to $32.1\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the MOF incorporated single fibrous membrane, Cu/Tb SNFs. Importantly, the surface area of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs $(61.3\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is found almost twice than that of the single fibrous membrane, indicating that side-by-side nanofibers can contribute to adsorption of a higher degree of particulate matters. Figure 4b shows the pore size distribution of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs. The average pore diameter is $6.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ for major sample, indicating the sample has a mesoporous structure [39]. Table 2 summarizes the surface area and pore size of PAN NFs, Cu/Tb SNFs and Cu//Tb SBS-NFs. The pore size of PAN NFs is observed decreased after the incorporation of MOFs. It is also found that the high exposure of MOFs further reduced the pore size distribution. Therefore, Cu//Tb SBS-NFs which have the smallest pore size distribution among others could have better filtration performance than PAN NFs and Cu/Tb SNFs.
All membranes were prepared randomly on nonwoven structures. The filtration efficiency of PAN NFs and Cu/Tb SNFs are $84.9\pm0.4$ and $83.3\pm0.2\%$ , respectively (Figure 4c). Compared with PAN NFs, Cu/Tb SNFs have a similar filtration efficiency but show a significantly lower pressure drop at the same basis weight. PAN NFs have more nanofibers and a denser structure than that of Cu/Tb SNFs, thus, it exhibits a higher pressure drop. Despite the low nanofiber amount, Cu/Tb SNFs shows a filtration efficiency similar to that of PAN NFs due to the electrostatic interaction and roughness of the Cu-MOF on the surface of Cu/Tb SNFs. Cu//Tb SBS-NFs have a higher surface area and smaller pore diameter distribution due to higher exposure of the MOFs than Cu/Tb SNFs, and it exhibits the filtration efficiency $90.2\pm0.3\%$ . Furthermore, the pressure drop of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs decreases to $60.7\pm0.9\mathrm{Pa}$ from the $90.1\pm$ 1.1 Pa for PAN NFs. This value is considerably lower than those of conventional filters [40]. The quality factor of the PAN NFs is 0.021 (Figure 4d). However, the quality factor of $\mathrm{Cu//Tb}$ SBS-NFs significantly increases to 0.038, showing a superior performance with approximately $80\%$ improvement. The increased diameter of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs results in a slight improvement (approximately $11\%$ ) compared with that of Cu/Tb SNFs. To investigate the effects of the CuMOF and Tb-MOF on the filtration performance, filtration tests of the side-by-side nanofibrous membranes with different MOF contents were carried out (Figure S4). Herein, the two MOFs show improved filtration efficiency, and only the Cu-MOF exhibits a reduced pressure drop. This means that the Cu-MOF has a greater effect on the overall filtration performance.
