Table 3 shows a comparison of the PM filtration performance between the Cu//Tb SBS-NFs and other reported materials. The Cu//Tb SBS-NFs show excellent filtration efficiency with a relatively low-pressure drop. However, all other reported materials had superior filtration efficiency with a high-pressure drop. The results further show that the Cu//Tb SBS-NFs reported in this work have comparable quality factors compared to previous reported materials, in addition, it overcomes the disadvantages of high-pressure drop in nanofibrous filter application.
## 3.3. Long-term performance
The mechanical property is one of the most important parameters to consider when using a membrane as an air filter. The tensile strengths and Young’s moduli of the materials are shown in Figure 5a. Cu//Tb SBS-NFs exhibit a tensile strength of $3.79\pm0.12\mathrm{~M~}$ Pa and Young’s modulus of $41.75\pm8.45$ MPa which are significantly higher than the other samples. The Young’s moduli of the membranes with MOFs are found increased compared with that of PAN NFs, suggesting the increased stiffness of PAN fibers after the MOF incorporation. However, the tensile strength of Cu/Tb SNFs is shown significantly lower than PAN NFs. This means that an excessive interaction between the polymer and MOF inhibits the ordering of the polymer and MOF when the content of the particles exceeds certain limits [44-47]. However, in the case of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs, mechanical properties increased without reaching a certain limit of particle concentration. Herein, there is an effective load transfer from the polymer to the MOFs. This indicates a uniform dispersion of the MOFs and their excellent interfacial adhesion with the polymer. When the MOF content allowed to deposit on only one side of SBS, it shows a sharp decrease in mechanical properties due to asymmetry on both sides (Figure S5). Overall improved mechanical properties of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs are attributed to the synergistic effect of side by side fibers and subsequent modification by the MOF. The thermal stability up to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was confirmed considering that the temperature of car exhaust gas and the exhaust port to be used in the field test ranged from 50 to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 5b). Cu//Tb SBS-NFs show negligible degradation of about $2\%$ up to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which confirms the major sample has excellent thermal stability. The weight loss of the samples up to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is attributed to a loss of adsorbed water and ethanol below [48].
Cu//Tb SBS-NFs membrane was fixed in self-designed filter holders to test PM filtration from car exhaust (Figure S6). The filtration efficiency of PM with various particle sizes from car exhaust is shown in Figure 5c. The removal efficiencies of PM particles with sizes of 0.3-1.0,
2.5-5.0, and $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ are 90.1-91.7, 96.1-96.7, and $100\%$ , respectively. Besides, high filtration efficiency still maintained even though the car exhaust was filtered for 30 hours (Figure 5d). The filtration efficiency increase with filtration time as a filter cake builds up, which acted as an additional filtration barrier [49, 50]. For this reason, the removal efficiency of PM10 achieved $100\%$ after 30 hours. For comparative purposes, the long-term and field tests of Cu/Tb SNFs were also carried out (Figure S7). Data confirm that filter cake accumulated faster in Cu/Tb SNFs than Cu//Tb SBS-NFs.
In addition to filtration efficiency, maintaining a high airflow is another important parameter for assessing the performance of an air filter. In Figure 5e, the air penetration through Cu//Tb SBS-NFs was demonstrated by car exhaust being able to flow at a velocity of $2{-}3~\mathrm{m/s}$ . The angle of a ribbon attached to the end of the pipe gradually decreased, but it still showed the great penetration of air through Cu//Tb SBS-NFs. The decrease of the angle indicates that the filter cake built up and the pressure drop increases.
