## 3.4. Luminescent property
It is usually well known that the luminescence of lanthanide cations often suffers from weak light absorption and the spin- or parity-forbidden f-f transition. However, in the case of TbMOF, the ligand acts as an antenna to absorb the energy from UV light $(254~\mathrm{nm})$ , and then transfers energy to the $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion, leading to a bright green characteristic color that can be readily observed with the naked eye. This is known as the “antenna effect” [51, 52]. The emission spectra of Tb-MOF are dominated by four characteristic bands at 486, 543, 582, and $618~\mathrm{nm}$ upon excitation at $260~\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the $\mathrm{{}^{5}D_{3}\rightarrow{}^{7}F_{n}}$ transitions $(\mathtt{n}=6,5,4$ and 3) for the $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion. The luminescence properties of the Tb-MOF were used to examine the PM adsorption process. The luminescence spectra of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs over adsorption time were investigated (Figure 6). The relative intensity of the four characteristic bands of Tb-MOF linearly decreased with increasing PM adsorption time. This may occur because PM was adsorbed to Cu//Tb SBS-NFs and prevented the ligand of the Tb-MOF from absorbing energy, so $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions could not receive energy This meant that the “antenna effect” from the ligand to the $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion was inhibited by PM. Finally, after 30 hours, $42\%$ of the initial luminescence intensity was observed. After 20 hours, the rate of decrease in luminescence intensity was slightly reduced because the increasing amount of PM adsorbed on the filter created a repulsive force between the passing PM and the adsorbed PM. As such, these results show that the reduced luminescence peak of Cu//Tb SBS-NFs is due to chemical effects as well as the physical effects of PM adsorption. SEM analysis was also performed to see any changes in the morphology of the nanofibers after the filtration test. The result shows the nanofibers of $30\mathrm{h}$ exposure are observed tangled, stuck, and formed the filter cake after the test (Figure S8). This could explain the reduced luminescence intensity of the fiber following the 30 hrs filtration.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we propose a novel side-by-side nanofibrous (SBS-NFs) membrane with improved filtration performance that allows the PM adsorption process can be observed with the naked eye. This Cu//Tb dual metal organic frameworks (MOF) - incorporated SBS-NFs membrane was fabricated via electrospinning using a self-designed nozzle for the first time. Compared with pure PAN nanofibers membrane, Cu//Tb dual MOF incorporated SBS-NFs (Cu//Tb SBS-NFs) membrane showed an enhanced filtration efficiency $(90.2\%)$ and a reduced pressure drop $(60.7\mathrm{Pa})$ . Low-pressure drop and high-quality factor (0.038) achieved for $\mathrm{Cu//Tb}$ SBS-NFs in this study suggest that it can overcome the disadvantage of nanofibrous filters reported elsewhere [13, 41, 43]. In the field test using car exhaust, PM2.5 and PM10 were removed each $590\%$ and $59\%$ for a long time (30 hours). The Cu-MOF contributed to improving filtration efficiency by reducing the pressure drop while Tb-MOF contributed to recognize the PM adsorption process of the SBS-NFs membrane through the changes in luminescent intensities that can be judged with the naked eyes. This result is important to answer when the air filter needs replacing. Although conceptually stimulating, all experiments were conducted at lab bench-scale and with simplified testing conditions. Therefore, pushing these new concepts into real-world practical applications with cost-effectiveness will be considered in the future.
